54 cases, run against one healthy system and 4 broken ones.
A layer fails if it drops more than 8% below the healthy run.

                                  det   grnd  judge   abst   retr   whse   build
  baseline (the healthy run)      83%   100%    70%    80%    86%    92%   —
  retrieval ranking reversed      76%   100%    50%    80%    79%    92%   RED
  retrieval starved to one p      74%   100%    60%    85%    57%    92%   RED
  warehouse answers the prev      70%   100%    70%    80%    86%    67%   RED
  refusal instruction remove      85%   100%    80%    80%    86%    92%   green

  bugs the suite caught     3 of 4
  bugs it slept through     1

  retrieval ranking reversed
    caught by judge — judge fell 20%
  retrieval starved to one passage
    caught by deterministic, judge, retrieval — retrieval fell 29%
  warehouse answers the previous quarter
    caught by deterministic, warehouse — warehouse fell 25%

  survived:
    refusal instruction removed from the prompt
      nothing dropped anywhere — the largest fall on any layer was 0%
      which is the cheap case: either the mutation is not a bug, or it is one
      no layer here looks at. Read the mutation before deciding which.

  Those are the only two possibilities, and the size of the largest drop
  is what separates them. A tighter band would catch the second kind and
  make the build fail on ordinary variance; a new layer catches it and
  leaves the rest alone. Every layer in the table above arrived that way.

  Note the refusal row. Taking the sentence about refusing out of the
  prompt moved abstention by 0%.

  Which is to say: barely at all, on this run. The refusals were not only
  coming from that sentence: the warehouse raises an error naming what it
  does hold, and the passages that come back plainly do not answer — and
  the model reports both. One run cannot say whether the sentence never
  matters or only sometimes does, which is exactly why the slice is scored
  on its own and a surviving mutant is worth rerunning.

This is mutation testing, and it is the only evidence that a green suite means
anything. A suite that has never been red is not passing — it is untested, and
the difference is invisible until the day it matters.

Three versions of this suite were written before the table above, and each
revision was dictated by a bug that walked past the one before it.

  three layers          caught one of four. The missing ones were a floor on
                        refusals, which an overall average had absorbed, and
                        one on retrieval, which a warehouse fallback hid.
  absolute floors       failed its own control on the next run, because every
                        layer is a sample and a floor an inch under today's
                        score goes red on Tuesdays. Hence the baseline.
  five layers           let the warehouse bug through: it moved the overall
                        figure six points and sat inside the band, so the
                        warehouse cases got a layer of their own.

Nobody designs the right suite first. You find out what it is missing by
breaking things on purpose and watching what fails to notice.
